---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import MamCode from "../../components/MamCode.astro";
import { examples } from "../../data/examples";
import { release, url } from "../../site";

const hello = examples[0];
// Everything below restates the "Install", "Build from source" and "VS Code"
// sections of the compiler README.
---

<Base
  title="Install"
  section="install"
  description={`Install Mammoth ${release.version} on Windows, or build it from source.`}
>
  <header class="page-head wrap">
    <p class="page-kicker">Get started</p>
    <h1>Install Mammoth</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      The Windows installer includes its own Java runtime, so you don't need
      Java or Rust, and it doesn't need administrator rights.
    </p>
    <div class="download">
      <a class="btn btn-primary btn-large" href={release.setup.url}>
        Download for Windows
      </a>
      <p class="download-meta">
        Mammoth {release.version} · 64-bit Windows · {release.setup.size}
        <br />
        or the <a href={release.zip.url}>portable zip</a> ({release.zip.size}) ·
        <a href={release.page}>release notes</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="status-note">
      <strong>{release.status}.</strong> The language, its standard library and
      the compiler's error messages may change between releases. The test
      suites run on Windows with Java 17. Linux and macOS have not been tested.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="steps wrap">
    <section class="step" aria-labelledby="step-1">
      <span class="step-num" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
      <div>
        <h2 id="step-1">Run the installer</h2>
        <p>
          Run <code>{release.setup.name}</code>. A setup window lets you pick
          the install folder and choose whether to add Mammoth to
          <code>PATH</code>. By default it installs for your user account in
          <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Mammoth</code>, and you can uninstall it
          from <strong>Settings → Apps</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rather not run a setup program? Unzip the
          <a href={release.zip.url}>portable zip</a> and double-click
          <code>install.cmd</code> inside it, or run <code>bin\mammoth.exe</code>
          straight from the folder.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="step" aria-labelledby="step-2">
      <span class="step-num" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
      <div>
        <h2 id="step-2">Check it works</h2>
        <p>Open a new terminal, then run:</p>
        <MamCode
          lang="powershell"
          code={'mammoth --version\nmammoth "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\\Programs\\Mammoth\\examples\\greet.mam"'}
        />
        <p>
          This compiles and runs the example, which prints
          <code>Hello, Maya!</code> and <code>Hello, Sam!</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="step" aria-labelledby="step-3">
      <span class="step-num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
      <div>
        <h2 id="step-3">Write your first program</h2>
        <p>
          Save this as <code>{hello.file}</code>, or
          <a href={url(`examples/${hello.file}`)} download>download it</a>:
        </p>
        <MamCode code={hello.code} file={hello.file} />
        <p>Then run it from the same folder:</p>
        <MamCode lang="powershell" code={`mammoth ${hello.file}`} />
        <p>
          Next, take the <a href={url("tour/")}>language tour</a> or browse the
          <a href={url("docs/")}>documentation</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <div class="install-more wrap">
    <section aria-labelledby="vscode">
      <h2 id="vscode">VS Code</h2>
      <p>
        Install the Mammoth Language VSIX with
        <strong>Extensions → Install from VSIX</strong>, open a saved
        <code>.mam</code> file, and use the play button or
        <strong>Mammoth: Run Mammoth File</strong>. Compiler diagnostics appear
        in the Problems panel.
      </p>
      <p>
        The extension's source is in
        <a href={`${release.repo}/tree/main/editors/vscode`}>editors/vscode</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="java">
      <h2 id="java">Choosing a Java</h2>
      <p>Mammoth uses the first Java it finds, in this order:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>the folder in <code>MAMMOTH_JAVA_HOME</code></li>
        <li>the runtime installed with Mammoth</li>
        <li><code>JAVA_HOME</code></li>
        <li><code>java</code> on <code>PATH</code></li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Set <code>MAMMOTH_JAVA_HOME</code> to a JDK to use a different Java,
        such as Java 21 for Minecraft mods.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="source">
      <h2 id="source">Other systems: build from source</h2>
      <p>
        The installer is for 64-bit Windows only. Elsewhere, install Rust and a
        Java 17 or later JDK, clone the
        <a href={release.repo}>compiler repository</a>, and run these once from
        its folder in PowerShell:
      </p>
      <MamCode
        lang="powershell"
        code={"cargo build --release\n$env:Path = \"$(Join-Path $PWD 'target\\release');$env:Path\"\nmammoth .\\examples\\greet.mam"}
      />
      <p>Repeat the <code>PATH</code> line in each new terminal.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
